### Rationale
I tested five S-band structures on the rented B200. None of them clearly beat the parent. The submitted version (8-row, 128-thread CTAs for B·S ≤ 600) measured S −0.4%, which is within noise; it was the best of the five. What was learned:

- **1 CTA per SM with each CTA owning one contiguous chunk of rows:** S +18%. Address locality matters: each SM streaming its own region is much worse than one dense address front.
- **The same with tokens interleaved across CTAs:** S +7%. Giving one thread more rows makes it worse, not better.
- **64-thread CTAs:** +6 to +10%. **768-thread CTAs:** +1 to +9%.
- **Bulk L2 prefetch of the tiles later waves will read:** the 586-token workload got 9% slower. Extra early requests just congest the memory.

My reading: S-band time is not limited by CTA launches or a latency bubble. DRAM is already the bottleneck, and the traffic it carries is larger than the nominal bytes.

